  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthen workplace compliance and wages systems in alignment with international labour standards</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2023, Papua New Guinea (PNG) ratified ILO Convention C190, reaffirming its commitment to eliminating violence and harassment in the world of work. This Convention promotes dignity, equality, and safety at work, and complements national efforts to strengthen occupational safety and health (OSH) systems. By addressing workplace violence and harassment, C190 reinforces the foundation for safe and inclusive working environments, contributing to stronger compliance and worker protections across sectors.
With support from the ILO, the Government is currently developing a National OSH Policy. This work is ongoing, with the Office of State Solicitor (OSS) advising the Department of Labour and Industrial Relations (DLIR) to carry out broad consultations with all relevant stakeholders. Once consultations are completed, a final draft of the Bill&#x2014;alongside the OSH Policy and draft regulations&#x2014;will be submitted for legal clearance before being presented to Parliament.
To address the rising cost of living, the Government is re-establishing the Minimum Wages Board (MWB) to review and update the national minimum wage, which was last revised in 2016. With strong support from the Secretary&#x2019;s office, the Department is leading nationwide consultations to engage a wide range of stakeholders. The Terms of Reference for the MWB were endorsed at the most recent National Tripartite Consultative Council meeting.
A technical workshop on minimum wage policy has already been completed with ILO support. The workshop focused on ILO Convention 131, global best practices, and evidence-based policymaking. National consultations are now underway, and the ILO will continue to provide technical assistance as PNG moves toward determining a new minimum wage rate.
These actions highlight the Government&#x2019;s ongoing commitment to improving workplace safety, promoting fair wages, and ensuring decent work for all Papua New Guineans.</narrative>
  </description>
